  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Service-Disabled Veteran-Owned Small Business Set-Asides obligated $7.3B in FY2026 to date, distributed across 13,477 awards with an average award size of $542K. Obligations are down 16.0% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the dominant buyer with $5.9B obligated through SDVOSB set-asides so far this fiscal year. Construction and IT categories account for the largest shares of dollars, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $2.1B and Other Computer Related Services at $1.4B. Top contractors show concentration within a few firms, with V3GATE, LLC receiving $410M and FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. receiving $255M. Historical context shows FY2026 to date at $7.3B after a peak at $9.1B in FY23 and declines since then. These figures are contract obligations, and the current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. This snapshot highlights where federal SDVOSB spending is concentrated by agency, industry, and vendor, and it frames current activity against the last several fiscal years of obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are SDVOSB set-aside obligations across agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs leads SDVOSB obligations with $5.9B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense follows with $705M, and the top ten buying agencies together include Department of Homeland Security at $132M and Department of Transportation at $110M. The concentration at VA means a large share of the $7.3B total is tied to one agency."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors captured the most SDVOSB dollars?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction captured $2.1B and Other Computer Related Services captured $1.4B in FY2026 to date. V3GATE, LLC is the largest vendor with $410M, followed by FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. with $255M and SPREZZATURA MANAGEMENT CONSULTING, LLC with $214M. These industries and vendors account for a substantial portion of the $7.3B in obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
